# A spherical lamp: the first source that is not flat.
#
#   --integrator naive|path --renderer mt --threads 8 --seed 0
#   --samples_ppx 4096 --max_depth 4 --output_width 256 --output_height 192
#
# WHAT IT ISOLATES
#
# The same question as `direct_lighting.stage` — do `naive` and `path` agree — asked of a curved
# source. It is deliberately that scene with the panel swapped for a sphere and the camera left
# where it was, so the two sets of figures can be read against each other.
#
# WHY A SPHERE IS NOT A RECTANGLE WITH A DIFFERENT SHAPE
#
# A point drawn uniformly over the area of a sphere lands on the side facing away, and is dropped:
# emission is one-sided, so `cos θₗ ≤ 0` answers "no sample" before any density is computed. The
# share that survives is the cap the shaded point can see, and from a distance d it is exactly
#
#   (1 − r/d) / 2
#
# — a half at best, and worse the closer one stands. Measured on this scene, whose lamp has r = 1
# and hangs 3 above the floor: 32.7 % of the draws survive straight underneath it, 43.8 % from 8.5
# away, against 33.3 % and 44.1 % predicted. **Two draws in three are thrown away where the light
# matters most.**
#
# The rectangle has no equivalent of this, every point of a flat source facing the same way. It is
# why pbrt samples the cone a sphere subtends from the shaded point rather than its area, and it
# makes this the scene where that change will be read. A uniform-area draw that is merely *correct*
# here is only half the result; the other half is the waste above, which is the baseline to beat.

camera pin_hole
  pos 0.0 2.9 0.0
  look 0.0 0.0 0.0
  up 0.0 0.0 -1.0

scene

  object simple
    rectangle 12 12
    lambertian color 0.6 0.6 0.6

  # The lamp. A sphere emits through every point of its surface, so unlike a `rectangle` it needs
  # no rotation to be aimed: there is no face to choose.
  object transformed
    object simple
      sphere 1.0
      diffuse_light color 6.0 6.0 6.0
    transform {
      translate 0.0 3.0 0.0
    }
